# Product direction

- Build a Spanish-language, responsive internal article register.
- Keep the React/TypeScript frontend separate from the legacy WPF desktop application.
- This is the Portfolio build: no login, environment variables, or Supabase runtime. Persist data in IndexedDB.
- The article table has exactly four columns: Nombre, Categoría, Fecha de ingreso, Acciones (Ver, Editar, Borrar).

# Architecture and quality

- Keep UI components presentational where practical; persistence belongs in `src/lib/inventoryRepository.ts` and demo fixtures in `src/data/demo.ts`.
- Keep persistence and validation in `src/lib/inventoryRepository.ts`. Use shared camelCase models from `src/types.ts`.
- Validate required values, duplicate codes, duplicate category names, and unique non-empty serial numbers. Show useful Spanish errors.
- Set `createdAt` and `updatedAt` with the system time on creation; update only `updatedAt` when editing.
- Keep keyboard focus, semantic labels, and reduced-motion support intact.

# Local data and security

- Migrate existing `localStorage` demo records once into IndexedDB; map `department` to `location` and `price` to `cost`.
- Browser data stays local to that browser profile and does not sync across devices.
- Files under `supabase/` are historical references only. Do not connect to or change remote Supabase projects from Portfolio.

# Workflow

- Keep dependencies minimal and the package lockfile synchronized.
- Run `npm run build` after TypeScript or UI changes.
- When layout or visual tokens change, update `docs/figma-brief.md`.
- Do not commit secrets, generated build output, or `node_modules`.
